      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beaufort%2C_Missouri"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1887 the railroad reached the area, prompting the entire community to move to its current spot at the crossroads of US 50 and Route 185.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        7,
        18,
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beaufort%2C_Missouri"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The town’s name comes from Beaufort, South Carolina, the native home of an early postmaster who helped establish the community.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        3,
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://kids.kiddle.co/Beaufort,_Missouri"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Beaufort sits at the intersection of two major highways, US 50 and Route 185, making it a natural crossroads in western Franklin County.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12,
        13,
        18,
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beaufort%2C_Missouri"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "As an unincorporated community, Beaufort has no municipal government of its own, relying on county administration for local matters.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11,
        17,
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beaufort%2C_Missouri"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Beaufort lies about 11 miles west of Union, placing it within easy reach of the county’s larger town while retaining its quiet character.",
